## Shaping the story

A video is watched once, often without sound at first, by someone who did not ask for it. Settle the story before the beats:

- **Who it is for.** A new developer, the product team, a reviewer: it sets what they already know and which words need explaining.
- **The one thing to remember.** One sentence. Every beat serves it; leave out what does not, however true.
- **The shape.** One of:
  - Problem, how it works, outcome ("problem"): the first beat names the problem the flow solves, the middle beats walk through the flow, and the last shows what the user has at the end. For most flows.
  - Before and after ("before-after"): the first beats show how it worked before and what was wrong with it, then what changed, and the last shows what is better now. For a change, a pull request or a migration.
  - The happy path, then what goes wrong ("failure"): the first beats show the flow working, then where it can fail and how it recovers, and the last shows that the user is safe either way. For retries, errors and edge cases.
  - The whole system, then one part ("zoom"): the first scene shows the whole system at a glance, then a second scene looks closely at the one part the video is about. For a part that only makes sense in its place.
- **The hook.** The first beat's line says, in under 5 seconds (about 12 words), the problem or the question the video answers, so the viewer wants the rest.
- **The length.** Aim for 45 to 90 seconds. Longer is fine for a longer flow, split into scenes.

## storyboard.json: the story

```ts
type Storyboard = {
  version: 1;
  scenes: Scene[];   // 1–3 items
};
type Scene = {
  id: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
  title: string;   // Shown as "1 · Title". ≤ 60 characters
  nodes: string[];   // The graph nodes shown in this scene. ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$; 1–12 items
  beats: Beat[];   // 3–10 items
};
type Beat = {
  id: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
  caption: string;   // The step caption shown on screen. ≤ 60 characters
  say?: string;   // The narration line. Without it the beat is timed by its actions and caption. ≤ 300 characters
  actions: Action[];
  notes?: string;   // The author's own notes; never shown.
};
type Action = AppearAction | FlowAction | HighlightAction | CalloutAction | DimAction | BadgeAction;
// The node rises into the scene.
type AppearAction = {
  type: "appear";
  node: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
};
// The edge draws in and carries a moving dot.
type FlowAction = {
  type: "flow";
  edge: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
  label?: string;   // ≤ 60 characters
};
// The node pulses to draw attention.
type HighlightAction = {
  type: "highlight";
  node: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
};
// A card with text next to the node, shown until the narration ends.
type CalloutAction = {
  type: "callout";
  node: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
  title?: string;   // ≤ 40 characters
  text: string;   // ≤ 140 characters
};
// Everything but these nodes fades back from here to the next dim or the next beat without one; a dim in a scene's last beat holds to its end.
type DimAction = {
  type: "dim";
  except: string[];   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$; at least 1
};
// A small status chip on the node.
type BadgeAction = {
  type: "badge";
  node: string;   // ≤ 40 characters; matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$
  text: string;   // ≤ 16 characters
  tone: "info" | "ok" | "warn" | "error";
};
```

- Scene and beat ids are unique across the whole storyboard.
- A scene's `nodes` are graph node ids. Its beats' actions may only name those nodes, and a flow's edge must join two of them.
- A node without an `appear` is there from the scene's start.

## Telling the story

- One idea per beat: one step of the flow, said once.
- The caption names the step; the narration explains why it matters. Write narration as you would say it, in short sentences. It is read at about 2.5 words a second, and that sets the beat's length; a beat without narration lasts at least its caption's length at 15 characters a second.
- Show before you tell: `appear` a node or `flow` an edge in the beat that first talks about it.
- Use `callout` for the one detail a viewer must remember, `badge` for a result, `highlight` to point, and `dim` to focus a beat on a few nodes (the focus lasts that beat; dim again in the next beat to keep it).
- Use scenes for the parts of a longer story, each with the nodes it needs. One scene is often enough.
- End on the outcome: show what the user has at the end of the flow.

